{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"alias":"lucky-egoistic-akitainu","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Summary\n\n`ready_tickets` is bounded at 90,000 bytes by bug `daughterly-agitative-ocelot`\n(`494b-2dd3-e9d3-4fb0`), but even after that fix's lean projection it sits at **58 rows /\n87,893 wire bytes — roughly ONE row of headroom**. The bound is correct and its refusal is\nactionable (it points at `next_batch`), but the tool will begin refusing its default call\nabout as soon as one more ticket becomes ready. That is a cliff, not a fixed problem.\n\n## The question this ticket answers\n\nDoes a DISCOVERY surface need near-full ticket rows at all? `ready_tickets` answers \"what can\nI work on next\". Measured on the real store (58 ready rows, 90,000-byte budget):\n\n| shape | bytes/row | rows that fit | headroom vs today |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| as shipped (lean, `TicketStateOut`) | 1,515 | 59 | +1 |\n| lean minus `file_impact`/`deps`/author/env fields | 801 | 112 | +54 |\n| summary fields, still via `TicketStateOut` | 572 | 157 | +99 |\n| summary fields via a NEW closed output model | 234 | 384 | +326 |\n\nTwo findings drive this:\n\n1. **The lean ready row still carries fields a discovery caller cannot use.** `file_impact` is\n   252 B/row (18.1% of the row) and `deps` 160 B/row (11.5%) — 30% of the payload between them,\n   for a question that only needs id/alias/title/type/status/priority and blocking state.\n2. **`TicketStateOut`'s declared defaults set a floor.** Projecting to summary fields but\n   returning `TicketStateOut` still costs 572 B/row, because `model_validate` re-adds\n   `description: null`, `comments: []`, `deps: []`, `inbound_deps: []`, `file_impact: []`,\n   `file_impact_scope`, `no_file_impact_reason`, `plan_review_health: null` and\n   `cross_session_warning: null`. **338 of those 572 bytes are re-added defaults** — the same\n   mechanism that made the parent bug's budget under-estimate by 178%. Reaching 234 B/row\n   requires a different, closed output model, which changes the tool's `outputSchema`.\n\n## Scope decision this ticket must make\n\nThe cheap half (row 2 of the table) is a further narrowing along the axis\n`494b-2dd3-e9d3-4fb0` ALREADY declared breaking, so it folds into that existing release note\nand needs no schema change. The large half (row 4) changes the declared `outputSchema` and\ninteracts with `full=True`, which must still return the whole shape — so the tool would carry\ntwo result types. `docs/api-stability.md` treats declared output schemas as a strong contract,\nso that half deserves its own decision rather than riding along.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A decision is recorded on whether `ready_tickets` returns a discovery-shaped result via a\n      new closed output model, or is merely narrowed further within `TicketStateOut`, with the\n      `full=True` behaviour stated for whichever is chosen.\n- [ ] Default `ready_tickets` has headroom for at least 3x the current ready count on this\n      store, measured on the wire payload (post-`model_validate`, post-`js_safe_result`), not on\n      raw reducer rows.\n- [ ] Any further narrowing of a default output is recorded in `docs/release-notes.md` and\n      `CHANGELOG.md` — folded into the existing `494b` entry if it is the same axis, or as its\n      own entry if the declared `outputSchema` changes.\n- [ ] `list_tickets` is explicitly considered and the asymmetry justified in the change: it has\n      six filter arguments, so an over-budget refusal there is actionable and the budget is a\n      guardrail rather than a cliff; `ready_tickets` has none.\n- [ ] Regression coverage asserts the per-row size ceiling, so the row cannot silently re-grow.","id":"0c99-8f2b-0de7-48f2","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"story","title":"Give ready_tickets a discovery-shaped result so its budget stops being a cliff"},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1787953792470677001,"uuid":"58b9d579-7147-4406-a8e6-b48ce56d6c91"}